1 DEASSIGN
    The DEASSIGN command deletes a logical name.  One trailing colon is
    removed from the logical name, so to delete a name that ends in a
    colon, give two.

    Syntax:

         DEASSIGN logical-name
         DEASSIGN/ALL

    Qualifiers:

        /PROCESS, /JOB, /GROUP, /SYSTEM, /TABLE=table

            The table to delete from.  The default is the process table.
            Deleting a table's name from LNM$PROCESS_DIRECTORY or
            LNM$SYSTEM_DIRECTORY deletes the table and all its descendants:

                 DEASSIGN/TABLE=LNM$PROCESS_DIRECTORY TAX

        /USER_MODE, /SUPERVISOR_MODE, /EXECUTIVE_MODE

            Delete names at this access mode and any less privileged
            mode.  The default is supervisor mode, so the executive-mode
            names SYS$INPUT, SYS$OUTPUT, SYS$ERROR and SYS$COMMAND are not
            deleted.

        /ALL

            Delete every such name in the table.
